from science_completion import q,p,m
QUESTIONS=[
q(1,'What is an independent variable?','The variable deliberately changed or selected by the investigator.','WS2.2'),
q(1,'What is instrument resolution?','The smallest change the instrument can distinguish.','WS3.7'),
q(1,'What does accuracy mean?','Closeness to the true value.','WS3.7'),
q(1,'What type of error makes every reading consistently too high by the same amount?','Systematic error.','WS3.7'),
q(1,'Which meter is connected in series to measure current?','An ammeter.','8.2.3–8.2.4'),
q(1,'What must be subtracted from loaded spring length to calculate extension?','The unloaded length.','8.2.6'),
q(1,'What is the independent variable when comparing the effect of insulation thickness?','Insulation thickness.','8.2.2'),
q(2,'Distinguish repeatability from reproducibility.','- **Repeatability:** the same investigator and conditions give similar repeated results.\n- **Reproducibility:** different investigators or equipment give similar results.','WS3.7'),
q(2,'Why does taking a mean of repeats help with random error but not with an unchanged zero offset?','- Random fluctuations can partly cancel, reducing their effect on the **mean**.\n- A zero offset shifts all readings systematically, so the mean **retains the offset** unless corrected.','WS3.7'),
q(2,'Why should an anomalous reading not be discarded automatically?','- It may be real, or indicate a cause that needs investigation.\n- **Repeat and check the method**; exclude it only for a justified measurement failure, recording the reason.','WS3.7'),
q(2,'A student tests longer wires but also uses thicker wire for the longest lengths. Why is this not a valid length-only comparison?','- **Cross-sectional area also changes resistance**.\n- The result cannot isolate length; keep material, area and temperature constant.','WS2.2;8.2.3'),
q(2,'Why is a floating object unsuitable for a simple displacement-volume method unless the method is adapted?','- It is not **fully submerged**, so it displaces less than its full volume.\n- Submerge it using a suitable adapted method and account for any extra object used.','8.2.5'),
q(2,'Why should a resistance-wire circuit be switched off between measurements?','- Current **heats the wire**.\n- Temperature changes resistance, so cooling helps keep a **control variable constant** and reduces burn risk.','8.2.3'),
q(2,'In an insulation comparison, give two important controls besides the insulation itself.','- Same **water mass and starting temperature**.\n- Same **container, lid and room conditions**, with matched measurement intervals.','8.2.2'),
q(2,'Explain why resistance is calculated from both voltage and current rather than current alone.','- **R = V/I**.\n- Current also depends on applied voltage, so comparing I alone at different V would not isolate resistance.','8.2.3'),
q(2,'Why does measuring several wavelengths improve a ripple-tank wavelength estimate?','- The same absolute ruler uncertainty is a **smaller fraction of a longer measured distance**.\n- Divide that distance by the number of **complete wavelengths** to find λ.','8.2.8'),
q(2,'For a fixed resistor, a current–voltage graph has current on y and voltage on x. What does its straight-line gradient represent?','- Gradient = **I/V**.\n- It is **1/R**, not R, for a constant-temperature ohmic resistor.','8.2.4'),
q(2,'Give one suitable way to reduce parallax in the spring practical and explain it.','- Read the pointer and ruler **at eye level**.\n- This reduces apparent displacement from viewing at an angle.','8.2.6'),
q(2,'Why is a microphone/data-logger method more suitable than a handheld stopwatch for a sound travel-time delay of 0.003 s?','- Human reaction time is much larger than the short delay.\n- Electronic timing can **resolve the delay** with much smaller timing error.','WS2.3;8.2.8'),
q(2,'What is the difference between a hazard and risk?','- A **hazard** is a potential source of harm.\n- **Risk** considers the likelihood and seriousness of harm in the actual situation.','WS1.5;WS2.4'),
q(2,'Explain why a strong correlation alone does not prove causation.','- Another uncontrolled variable or biased sample could produce the relationship.\n- Further **controlled evidence and a plausible mechanism** are needed before attributing a cause.','WS1.1;WS3.5–3.7'),
q(3,'Three repeated times are 1.8, 2.0 and 1.9 s. Calculate the mean, range and half-range uncertainty estimate.','- Mean = (1.8 + 2.0 + 1.9)/3 = **1.9 s**.\n- Range = 2.0 − 1.8 = **0.2 s**.\n- Half-range estimate = **±0.1 s**, with other sources of uncertainty considered separately.','WS3.3–3.4'),
q(3,'An empty cylinder has mass 80 g. With 50 cm³ of liquid it has mass 120 g. Calculate liquid density.','- Liquid mass = 120 − 80 = **40 g**.\n- ρ = m/V = 40/50 = **0.80 g/cm³**.\n- This equals **800 kg/m³**.','8.2.5'),
q(3,'A spring measures 12.0 cm unloaded and 15.0 cm under a 6.0 N load. Calculate extension and spring constant within the proportional region.','- Extension = 15.0 − 12.0 = **3.0 cm = 0.030 m**.\n- k = F/e = 6.0/0.030.\n- **k = 200 N/m**.','8.2.6'),
q(3,'A heater supplies 8,000 J to a 0.40 kg block, raising its temperature by 20°C. Calculate specific heat capacity using the measured input.','- **c = E/(mΔθ)**.\n- c = 8,000/(0.40 × 20).\n- **1,000 J/kg°C**, assuming the input energy heats the block.','8.2.1'),
q(3,'Explain why heat loss to the room generally makes a metal-block specific-heat result too high when all electrical input is used in the calculation.','- Not all supplied energy increases the **block’s thermal store**.\n- The measured temperature rise is smaller than the ideal rise.\n- In **c = E/(mΔθ)**, using all input E with that smaller Δθ overestimates c. Insulation reduces the error.','8.2.1'),
q(3,'Explain how to vary force while keeping total moving mass fixed in a trolley-and-hanger acceleration experiment.','- Transfer masses **from trolley to hanger** rather than adding new mass to the system.\n- Total moving mass stays the same, while hanging weight increases.\n- Measure acceleration for each driving force, keeping track and friction conditions comparable.','8.2.7'),
q(3,'A student compares IR readings from two cube faces, but the detector is closer to the second face. Evaluate the comparison.','- Detector reading depends on **distance**, not just surface finish.\n- The change makes the finish comparison invalid.\n- Use the **same distance and angle**, matched surface temperature, and repeat readings.','8.2.10','2023 2H Q01: related controls'),
q(3,'Describe how to check whether repeated reflection measurements support the reflection law.','- Measure incidence and reflection angles **from the normal**.\n- Repeat reflection readings and calculate the mean at each incidence angle.\n- Compare **mean r with i**, considering measurement uncertainty and protractor resolution.','8.2.9','2023 2H Q07.2–07.5'),
q(3,'Why does a filament lamp’s I–V graph curve even when its circuit is connected correctly?','- Increasing current heats the filament.\n- Higher temperature increases **resistance**.\n- Current then rises less rapidly with voltage, so the I–V gradient decreases.','8.2.4'),
q(3,'Explain why peer review and reproduction are useful before a scientific claim is widely accepted.','- Specialists check **methods, evidence and reasoning** during peer review.\n- Independent investigations test whether results are **reproducible**.\n- These steps can expose weaknesses, although neither guarantees a claim is permanently correct.','WS1.6;WS3.7'),
q(4,'Describe how to measure the density of an irregular solid that sinks and does not dissolve in water.','- Measure mass with a balance, checking its zero.\n- Record an initial water volume in a measuring cylinder or use a displacement can.\n- Fully submerge the solid, removing trapped bubbles, and measure **displaced volume**.\n- Calculate **ρ = m/V** in matching units; read the meniscus at eye level and repeat suitably.','8.2.5'),
q(4,'Describe how to investigate the I–V characteristic of a resistor.','- Connect ammeter in **series**, voltmeter **across the resistor** and an adjustable low-voltage supply.\n- Change voltage through a suitable range, recording paired V and I readings.\n- Obtain negative readings by reversing polarity where appropriate and repeat. Limit heating to keep temperature approximately constant.\n- Plot **I against V** and assess the best-fit line; a constant-temperature ohmic resistor gives a straight line through the origin.','8.2.4'),
q(4,'Explain how to measure wave speed on a stretched string using a stationary pattern.','- Use a vibration generator and signal generator with fixed string type and tension.\n- Adjust frequency until a clear stationary pattern forms; record **f**.\n- Measure several loop lengths. Each loop is **λ/2**, so find λ from the total distance.\n- Calculate **v = fλ**, repeat and keep hanging loads secure.','8.2.8'),
q(4,'A student says, “My readings repeat closely, so they must be accurate.” Evaluate this claim and suggest a check.','- Closely clustered readings show **precision and repeatability** under the same conditions.\n- They could all contain the same **systematic error**, such as a zero offset.\n- Check zero and calibration against a reference, or use an independent appropriate instrument.\n- Compare with the expected or reference value and its uncertainty before concluding accuracy.','WS3.7'),
q(6,'Plan an investigation of how insulation thickness affects cooling of water. Include controls, measurements and a useful conclusion.','- Use identical containers with different thicknesses of the **same insulation material**. Match the exposed area and use comparable lids.\n- Put the same mass of water in each, at the **same starting temperature**, and keep room conditions and draught exposure comparable.\n- Record temperature at fixed equal intervals for the same total time, using suitable thermometers in consistent positions.\n- Repeat each thickness and calculate means. Compare temperature drops over the same time or plot comparable cooling curves.\n- The thickness with the smaller drop reduces cooling more under those conditions. Use actual data to judge the trend and allow for uncertainty.\n- Handle hot water safely on heatproof surfaces. A valid comparison controls material and initial conditions rather than changing them alongside thickness.','8.2.2'),
q(6,'Describe a complete investigation of how wire length affects resistance. Include safety, controls and analysis.','- Connect a low-voltage supply and ammeter in series with a test wire. Connect a voltmeter across the selected length.\n- Use a ruler and movable contact to test several measured lengths, including repeated readings.\n- Keep **wire material, cross-sectional area and temperature** constant. Limit current and switch off between measurements to reduce heating.\n- At each length, measure **V and I**, then calculate **R = V/I**. Do not compare current alone if voltage varies.\n- Plot mean R against length with quantities and units on the axes. Assess whether the best-fit line supports a proportional relationship, considering uncertainty and contact resistance.\n- Check connections before switching on and avoid short circuits or hot wire. Give the observed trend with data rather than assuming every result must lie exactly on a line.','8.2.3'),
q(6,'Plan a trolley investigation of acceleration at different masses while keeping driving force approximately constant. Explain how the data test the relationship.','- Use a low-friction track, trolley, pulley, string and a fixed hanging mass. Secure the pulley and arrange a safe trolley stop.\n- Add mass to the trolley while keeping the **hanging mass fixed**. Record the total moving mass, including the hanger.\n- Use light gates or a motion sensor to measure velocities and time, then find **a = Δv/Δt** before the hanger reaches the floor.\n- Repeat each mass and calculate mean acceleration. Keep track, string and friction conditions comparable, measuring or allowing for friction where possible.\n- Plot acceleration against **1/total moving mass**. An approximately straight line supports inverse proportionality at fixed driving force.\n- Explain the approximation: driving weight acts on the whole moving system; string tension on the trolley alone is not equal to hanging weight during acceleration.','8.2.7')]

